// Details for the spreadsheet plate, part 5: collaboration, scripting, protection, files, external data, cross-cutting concerns.
ATLAS.details("spreadsheet", {
  "collab-session-sync": {
    s: "The connection protocol between clients and the server: join, send operations with the last known revision, receive transformed operations and acknowledgments, and catch up after gaps.",
    d: "A client opens a session, receives a snapshot and the current revision, and then sends each local operation tagged with the revision it was based on. The server assigns a global order, transforms the operation against any it had not seen, applies it, and broadcasts the result with its new revision. The client keeps a buffer of sent and unacknowledged operations and transforms incoming ones against that buffer before applying them. Heartbeats detect broken connections, and a reconnecting client resumes from its last acknowledged revision or reloads the snapshot. Batching groups quick edits into one message. The protocol must tolerate duplicates, reordering and partial failure.",
    k: ["Central server ordering versus peer-to-peer convergence: a server gives one total order and simple transforms, but needs availability and is a scaling point.", "One operation in flight versus pipelining several: one at a time keeps the client logic simple, while pipelining hides latency but needs a larger transform buffer."],
    p: ["A retried message that was already applied is applied twice unless operations carry unique ids.", "A client that reconnects after the log was truncated resumes from a revision that no longer exists."],
    v: "Drive two clients through random concurrent edits over a lossy connection with delays and reconnects, and confirm both converge to the server state and no operation is lost or applied twice.",
    dep: ["range-operational-transform", "op-log-and-snapshots"]
  },
  "range-operational-transform": {
    s: "Rewrites concurrent operations on cells, ranges, rows and columns against each other so they apply in any arrival order and every replica reaches the same state.",
    d: "Operations include set cell, set range, clear, insert and delete rows or columns, move, format, sort and merge. Transform functions take two concurrent operations and produce versions adjusted for each other. Cell edits at different addresses commute, an insert before a cell shifts that cell's address, a delete of a row makes edits to it vanish, and two inserts at the same position get a deterministic tie break. Range operations may need splitting into pieces. Formulas inside operations also carry references that must be shifted by concurrent structural edits. The number of pairs of operation types is large, and every pair needs a proven rule.",
    k: ["Operational transform with a central server versus replicated data types: OT keeps spreadsheet semantics like shifted addresses direct, but needs a transform for every pair of operation types.", "Splitting range operations into cell pieces versus transforming whole ranges: splitting is simple and correct, but expands one operation into thousands."],
    p: ["A transform pair that is not convergent causes replicas to diverge permanently and silently after a rare interleaving.", "Edits to a cell inside a row deleted by someone else are dropped, with no feedback to the author."],
    v: "Generate random pairs and triples of concurrent operations of all types, apply them in different orders on separate replicas, and confirm identical states, including formulas with shifted references, in every case.",
    dep: ["structural-edit-engine", "reference-adjustment-on-edit", "sparse-cell-storage"]
  },
  "op-log-and-snapshots": {
    s: "The durable ordered history of operations plus periodic full snapshots, from which any client or revision can be reconstructed.",
    d: "The server appends each accepted operation to a log with its revision number and author, and periodically writes a snapshot of the workbook at a given revision. A new client loads the latest snapshot and replays the tail of the log. Snapshots are compact binary forms of the model rather than the package format. Old log segments are compacted once every client has passed them and revision history no longer needs them. Storage must be durable before acknowledging, or the server may lose an edit it confirmed. Large pastes make large log records, so operations store compact range data.",
    k: ["Snapshot plus log tail versus the log alone: snapshots bound load time and log size, but cost writes and need consistency with the log position.", "Acknowledging after durable write versus after memory apply: durable acknowledgment never loses confirmed edits, but adds storage latency to every edit."],
    p: ["Compacting the log before a slow client has passed it forces that client into a full reload and loses its pending operations.", "A snapshot taken mid-operation, not at a clean revision, disagrees with the log and replay corrupts state."],
    v: "Kill the server mid-session, restart it, and confirm every acknowledged edit is present. Load a fresh client from snapshot and tail and compare its model with a client that has been connected the whole time.",
    dep: ["workbook-sheet-cell-model", "package-container"]
  },
  "conflict-resolution": {
    s: "Rules for edits that cannot both take effect, such as two users writing the same cell, deleting what another edited, or editing a protected cell, including how the outcome is shown.",
    d: "Transformation guarantees convergence but not intent. For the same cell, the usual rule is that the later operation in server order wins, and the loser's edit is overwritten. A cell edited concurrently with its row's deletion is lost. Sorting while another user edits a range moves rows under their cursor. Some designs lock a cell while it is in edit mode; others show a notice. Formula and value conflicts can be resolved at finer grain, such as per format property rather than per cell. The key design choice is when to tell users that their edit lost.",
    k: ["Last writer wins versus locking cells while edited: last writer wins never blocks anyone, but silently overwrites, while locks protect work and add blocking and stale lock cleanup.", "Per-property merging of formats versus whole-cell replacement: per-property merging keeps both users' intent, but needs a richer operation model."],
    p: ["Two people retype a number in the same cell and the later one wins, with no notice that the first was overwritten.", "A user's long edit in the cell editor is committed over a collaborator's formula change that arrived during the edit."],
    v: "Have two sessions write the same cell and delete its row at the same moment, and confirm both converge, the documented winner takes effect, and the losing user gets a visible indication.",
    dep: ["range-operational-transform", "collab-session-sync", "edit-bar-and-cell-editor"]
  },
  "presence-and-cursors": {
    s: "Shows each collaborator's name, selection and edit state in real time, as colored outlines and labels on the grid.",
    d: "Clients publish their active cell, selection ranges and edit mode over a lightweight channel that is not part of the document history. The server relays them to others, throttled, with a timeout to remove users who vanish. Positions must be transformed by concurrent structural edits, or a collaborator's cursor will appear in the wrong row after an insert. The renderer draws outlines and name flags in an overlay layer, with colors assigned per user. Presence also feeds features such as a lock indicator on a cell being edited and a follow mode that scrolls to another user's view. Presence data is ephemeral and must be cheap.",
    k: ["Ephemeral presence on a separate channel versus operations in the log: separate channels keep the history clean and fast, but need their own transform and timeout logic.", "Throttled updates versus every movement: throttling saves bandwidth, but cursors lag and jump."],
    p: ["Presence not adjusted after a row insert places collaborators' outlines over the wrong data.", "A crashed client leaves a ghost cursor on the sheet because no timeout is applied."],
    v: "With three sessions, move selections quickly, insert rows above them, and confirm every outline follows its cells. Kill one session and confirm its cursor disappears within the timeout.",
    dep: ["collab-session-sync", "selection-model", "cell-paint-pipeline"]
  },
  "comments-and-threads": {
    s: "Notes and discussion threads anchored to cells, with authors, replies, mentions, resolution state and notifications.",
    d: "A comment is attached to a cell, not to its value, so it must move with the cell through row insertion, sort and cut and paste. A thread has an ordered list of replies from different authors, a resolved flag and mentions that trigger notifications to named users. Comments are stored as workbook objects with ids and are edited through the same operation stream as cells, so they converge under concurrent edits. Deleting the anchor cell's row deletes or orphans the comment. The sheet shows an indicator, and a side panel lists threads. Permissions decide who may comment without being able to edit cells.",
    k: ["Anchoring to a cell address that moves with edits versus a stable cell id: address anchors reuse the existing edit machinery, but sorting and moves must remap them.", "Separate comment permission versus edit permission: separate rights suit reviewers, but add a role and per-operation checks."],
    p: ["Sorting a range moves values but leaves comments at the old rows, so they discuss different data.", "A mention notification is sent to someone who has no access to the workbook, leaking its title or content."],
    v: "Add a thread, reply from a second user with a mention, sort the range and insert rows, and confirm the thread stays with its cell and the mentioned user is notified only if they have access.",
    dep: ["range-operational-transform", "structural-edit-engine", "sharing-roles"]
  },
  "revision-history": {
    s: "Lets users browse earlier versions of the workbook, see who changed what, compare versions and restore one.",
    d: "Built on the operation log and snapshots, the history groups operations into sessions by author and time and offers named versions. Viewing an old revision reconstructs the model at that point from the nearest snapshot and the log. Comparison diffs two reconstructed models cell by cell and shows changes by author color. Restoring writes a new revision whose content equals the old one, rather than rewriting history, so collaborators see it as a normal edit. Retention policies limit how far back it goes. Reconstruction cost grows with the distance from a snapshot, so snapshot spacing sets the latency.",
    k: ["Restoring by appending a new revision versus truncating history: appending is safe and visible to collaborators, but history only grows.", "Grouping edits by session and time versus listing every operation: grouping is readable, but hides the individual changes that people want to find."],
    p: ["Restoring an old version overwrites work done by collaborators since then, with no warning to them.", "Retention that drops snapshots makes old revisions impossible to reconstruct while their log entries remain."],
    v: "Make edits as two users over a day of simulated time, open a revision from the middle, confirm it matches a dump taken then, and restore it and confirm other clients receive it as one new revision.",
    dep: ["op-log-and-snapshots", "workbook-sheet-cell-model", "collab-session-sync"]
  },
  "script-object-model": {
    s: "The programmatic interface to the workbook: objects for sheets, ranges, tables, charts and events, with methods that read and write through the same command path as the interface.",
    d: "A script gets a handle to the workbook and navigates to sheets and ranges. Range objects read and write values, formulas and formats in bulk, since per-cell calls across the sandbox boundary are slow. Writes go through the command layer, so they are undoable, validated, protected and collaborative. Reads return values, forcing a recalculation if needed. Batching calls into a single transaction avoids a recalculation per write. The object model hides the internal storage, which lets the engine change. Versioning matters: scripts written against it outlive releases, so the interface must be stable.",
    k: ["Bulk range operations versus per-cell access: bulk calls are orders of magnitude faster across a boundary, but make element-by-element scripts awkward.", "Writes through the command layer versus direct storage writes: the command layer keeps undo, protection and collaboration correct, but adds overhead."],
    p: ["A script that writes one cell at a time in a loop triggers a recalculation per write and takes minutes on a large workbook.", "Breaking changes to the object model silently break user scripts that cannot be found or tested centrally."],
    v: "Run a script that fills a hundred thousand cells in one bulk write and confirm a single recalculation and a single undo step. Run it as a user without edit rights and confirm it is refused.",
    dep: ["workbook-sheet-cell-model", "undo-redo", "sheet-protection"]
  },
  "script-sandbox": {
    s: "Runs user code in an isolated environment with limits on time, memory, network and file access, exposing only the workbook object model.",
    d: "Scripts run in a separate execution context, in-process isolate or separate process, with no direct access to the host's files, network or interface. A permission model lists what a script may call, such as network requests to approved hosts. Limits on CPU time, memory and output size stop runaway scripts, and calls into the model cross a controlled boundary. Server-side execution protects collaborators from code written by others, and unattended scripts run with a stored identity. The hard part is the boundary: every exposed function is an attack surface, and cancellation must work even when the script is in a tight loop.",
    k: ["Isolating in a separate process versus an in-process isolate: a process gives a hard boundary and killability, but costs startup time and each model call crosses a process boundary.", "Fixed resource limits versus user-configurable: fixed limits are safe, but stop legitimate long jobs."],
    p: ["A script with an infinite loop blocks the editing thread because the limit check runs only at function calls.", "A script requests an unapproved host through a redirect and exfiltrates workbook data."],
    v: "Run scripts that loop forever, allocate until exhaustion, read a file and call an unapproved host, and confirm each is terminated or denied with a clear error while the workbook stays responsive.",
    dep: ["script-object-model", "formulas-as-untrusted-code"]
  },
  "macro-recorder": {
    s: "Captures user actions as script statements that can be replayed, turning interface operations into readable code.",
    d: "While recording, the command layer emits each applied command as a script call against the object model: setting a range value, applying a format, inserting rows. Selections and navigation are recorded only where later steps depend on them. The output is editable code, though recorded scripts tend to be verbose, hard-coded to addresses and tied to the current selection. Replay executes the statements through the normal path and can run on other ranges if the user generalizes them. Not every interface action has a command-layer equivalent, so the recorder either skips such actions or marks a gap.",
    k: ["Recording commands versus recording raw input events: commands give stable, readable code, but miss behaviors that bypass the command layer.", "Absolute addresses versus relative to selection: absolute is repeatable, while relative is reusable but depends on where the cursor is."],
    p: ["A macro recorded with the cursor in one place does nothing useful when replayed from another.", "Actions without a command equivalent are skipped silently, so replay differs from what was recorded."],
    v: "Record a sequence of edits, formats and a sort, replay it on a fresh copy of the sheet and confirm the result matches the recorded one cell by cell, and the output is readable code.",
    dep: ["script-object-model", "undo-redo"]
  },
  "custom-functions": {
    s: "User-defined functions callable from cell formulas, written in the scripting language or as formula-defined functions, registered into the function registry.",
    d: "A custom function is registered with a name, parameter types and body. When a cell calls it, the engine evaluates the arguments and invokes the body through the sandbox, treating it as a pure computation: it receives values, returns a value or array, and may not write to the sheet. Calls may be asynchronous, for example to fetch data, which needs a pending state in the cell and a way to deliver the result later. Costs are high compared to built-ins, so results are cached by arguments, and calls from a filled column are batched. Their volatility and thread-safety must be declared.",
    k: ["Pure functions with no side effects versus functions that may write: purity allows caching and parallel evaluation, but rules out many macro-style uses.", "Batching calls from a filled range into one invocation versus one per cell: batching removes boundary cost, but complicates error handling per cell."],
    p: ["A custom function that reads another sheet's cell directly, not through arguments, has dependencies the graph does not know about and stays stale.", "An asynchronous function that never returns leaves cells pending forever."],
    v: "Register a custom function, fill it down a thousand rows, change an argument cell and confirm only affected rows recompute through one batched call. Make it hang and confirm a timeout error appears in the cell.",
    dep: ["function-registry", "script-sandbox", "formula-interpreter"]
  },
  "event-triggers": {
    s: "Hooks that run scripts when the workbook opens, a cell is edited, a form is submitted, or a schedule fires.",
    d: "Triggers register a handler for an event: an edit event with the changed range and old and new values, an open event, a time-driven schedule or an external request. Edit triggers run after the command is applied and receive a description of the change. Handlers run in the sandbox under a stored identity, with execution quotas. They may themselves write to the sheet, which fires further events, so loops need detection and depth limits. In a shared workbook, the question is where a handler runs and for which edits: once on the server, rather than once per collaborator's client.",
    k: ["Running triggers on the server once versus on every client: server execution is consistent and safe, but needs identity management and cannot touch local interface state.", "Synchronous handlers before commit versus asynchronous after: before-commit can veto edits, but adds latency and failure modes to every edit."],
    p: ["A handler that writes to the range it listens on fires itself indefinitely.", "A trigger running as the workbook owner lets any collaborator cause actions with the owner's rights."],
    v: "Install an edit trigger that logs changes and one that writes back, make edits from two clients, and confirm each fires exactly once per edit, a write-back loop is stopped at the depth limit, and the identity used is the documented one.",
    dep: ["script-object-model", "script-sandbox", "collab-session-sync"]
  },
  "sheet-protection": {
    s: "Locks cells and restricts actions such as formatting, inserting or deleting on a sheet, with an optional password, while leaving chosen cells editable.",
    d: "Each cell style carries a locked flag, true by default, which has an effect only when the sheet is protected. A protected sheet has a list of permitted actions such as selecting locked cells, formatting columns, sorting, or using filters. Every command checks the flags before it applies: an edit to a locked cell is refused. Structure commands and bulk operations need checks across all cells they touch, so a paste over a mixed range either fails entirely or skips locked cells. A password is stored as a hash in the file, which deters accidents but is not strong protection against someone with the file.",
    k: ["Checks inside the command layer versus in the interface: command-layer checks cover scripts, paste and collaboration, but need every command to declare the cells it touches.", "Password-hashed sheet protection versus real encryption: hashing stops casual edits, but anyone with the file can strip it."],
    p: ["A bulk command checks only the first cell and modifies locked cells deeper in the range.", "Users treat sheet protection as a security feature and share files that anyone can unprotect."],
    v: "Protect a sheet with some unlocked cells and try typing, pasting across both, sorting, inserting rows and running a script, and confirm each locked write is refused with an explanation and unlocked writes succeed.",
    dep: ["style-table", "undo-redo", "workbook-sheet-cell-model"]
  },
  "range-permissions": {
    s: "Grants specific people edit rights on named ranges of a protected sheet, so different collaborators can change different regions.",
    d: "A permission entry names a range and the users or groups that may edit it without unlocking the sheet. Checks combine the sheet's protection, the cell's locked flag, and the range's allow list. In a shared workbook, the server evaluates this for each incoming operation, using the author's identity, and rejects or trims operations that touch forbidden cells. Ranges move with structural edits, and an insert into a permitted range raises the question of whether the new cells are permitted. Permissions apply to scripts and to paste as well. Hiding formulas or whole sheets is a related feature and carries similar enforcement requirements.",
    k: ["Server-side enforcement on each operation versus client-side checks: server checks cannot be bypassed by a modified client, but cost latency and require partial acceptance rules.", "Inserted cells inherit the range's permission versus default locked: inheriting is what editors expect, but can grant rights over more cells than intended."],
    p: ["A client-only check lets a modified client send operations the server accepts for cells it should not touch.", "A row inserted at the edge of a permitted range is locked, and the editor cannot fill the new row."],
    v: "As a user with rights to one range, send edits to that range, outside it, and through paste and a script spanning both, and confirm only the permitted cells change and the server logs rejections.",
    dep: ["sheet-protection", "sharing-roles", "collab-session-sync"]
  },
  "sharing-roles": {
    s: "Assigns each person a role such as owner, editor, commenter or viewer, through direct invitation or link, and enforces it on every request.",
    d: "A workbook has an access list of identities with roles and optional link-based access with a role and expiry. The role determines which operation types the server accepts: viewers receive state but send no operations, commenters may add comments only, editors may change cells, and owners manage sharing. Roles are checked on the session join and on every operation. Revoking access must end open sessions promptly. Presence names, comment authorship and revision history expose identities, so the access model also governs what each person can see about others. Link sharing makes the link a bearer credential.",
    k: ["Role checks on every operation versus on session join only: per operation catches revoked and downgraded users, but costs a check per message.", "Anyone-with-link access versus named invitations: links are convenient, but anyone who obtains the link has the rights."],
    p: ["A user whose access was revoked stays connected and keeps editing, because roles are checked only on join.", "A viewer link is forwarded widely, and the workbook, including its history and comments, is readable by unknown people."],
    v: "Downgrade an editor to viewer during an open session and confirm their next operation is rejected and no further changes arrive from them. Revoke a link and confirm open sessions through it end.",
    dep: ["collab-session-sync", "revision-history"]
  },
  "file-encryption": {
    s: "Encrypts the saved package with a key derived from a password so that its contents cannot be read without it.",
    d: "The package bytes are encrypted with a symmetric cipher using a key derived from the password through a slow, salted key derivation function, and wrapped in an outer container that stores the salt, parameters and integrity check. Opening asks for the password, derives the key, verifies integrity and then decrypts, which in practice means the entire file must be read before any part can be parsed. Compared to sheet protection, this is genuine confidentiality. The cost is that lost passwords cannot be recovered, that partial and streaming access is lost, and that the algorithm parameters must be strong and upgradable.",
    k: ["Encrypting the whole package versus per part: whole-package hides structure and sizes, but forces full decryption before any read.", "A strong slow key derivation versus a fast one: slow derivation resists guessing, but adds delay at every open and drains weak devices."],
    p: ["A forgotten password makes the only copy permanently unreadable, and support cannot help.", "An old, weak legacy encryption mode is kept for compatibility and is breakable in minutes."],
    v: "Save with a password, confirm the file contains no readable text from the workbook, open with a wrong password and see a clear failure, flip one byte and see an integrity error, and open with the right password.",
    dep: ["package-container"]
  },
  "package-container": {
    s: "The zipped archive that holds a workbook as separate parts, with a manifest of content types and relationships linking parts together.",
    d: "A workbook file is a compressed archive of parts: one for the workbook structure, one per sheet, shared strings, styles, drawings, charts, tables, pivot caches, comments and external link caches. A content-types manifest says what each part is and relationship parts say how parts refer to each other by id, such as a sheet to its drawing. Writers lay out parts in a sensible order and compress each. Readers locate parts through relationships, not fixed paths. The archive's central directory allows random access, but reading in one streaming pass needs a different strategy. Size limits and zip bombs matter for untrusted files.",
    k: ["Many small parts with relationships versus one monolithic file: parts allow independent loading and extension, but add indirection and bookkeeping.", "Compressing each part separately versus the whole archive: per-part compression allows random access, at some cost in compression ratio."],
    p: ["A malicious archive that expands to gigabytes exhausts memory unless extraction size and ratio are limited.", "A writer that drops a relationship for a part it does not understand makes other programs report the file as corrupt."],
    v: "Save a workbook, list the archive and verify every part is declared in the manifest and reachable by relationships. Open a crafted archive with an enormous compression ratio and confirm it is rejected without allocating the full size.",
    dep: ["part-readers-and-writers"]
  },
  "part-readers-and-writers": {
    s: "Convert between the XML-style text of each package part and in-memory model objects, including cells, formulas, styles and drawings.",
    d: "Each part has a schema describing elements and attributes. The reader parses with an event-based parser, building the model without a full document tree, and maps stored values: numbers as text, shared string indexes, style indexes, formula strings in the neutral syntax, shared formula groups and cached results. The writer reverses this, emitting cells in row order. Differences between producers are the main difficulty: optional attributes, different defaults, undocumented extension elements and slight schema violations all occur in real files, and a reader that rejects them is unusable. Names, locale and notation conversion happen here.",
    k: ["A lenient reader that accepts deviations versus strict schema validation: lenient opens real-world files, but may accept corrupt data without a signal.", "Event-based parsing versus building a tree: events use little memory on large sheets, but make cross-referencing parts harder."],
    p: ["A reader that assumes an attribute default different from other producers' default shifts every column width or date base.", "Writing formulas in the display locale rather than the neutral syntax makes the file unreadable elsewhere."],
    v: "Load a corpus of files from different producers, save them again, reload, and compare the model before and after. Compare each cell value and formula against the original and report any differences.",
    dep: ["package-container", "formula-printer", "style-table", "string-interning"]
  },
  "streaming-load-and-save": {
    s: "Reads and writes huge sheets incrementally, one row at a time, so memory stays bounded and the first rows appear before the file has finished loading.",
    d: "A streaming reader pulls rows from the sheet part as the parser produces them, writing into block storage directly and updating progress. The interface can show the first screen while loading continues in the background, with the rest of the sheet marked as loading. A streaming writer emits rows from storage without building a document in memory, compressing as it goes. Streaming imposes order constraints: shared strings and styles must be known before the cells that use them, or placed to allow a second pass. Failures mid-stream must leave the file valid or clearly incomplete. Cancelling a load needs to unwind cleanly.",
    k: ["Streaming load into the live model versus loading fully before showing: streaming shows data sooner, but must handle edits and formulas that arrive while loading.", "Writing to a temporary file then renaming versus writing in place: temporary files protect against crashes, but need free space and a final rename."],
    p: ["A crash during an in-place save truncates the only copy of the workbook.", "Formulas reading rows that have not loaded yet compute with partial data and then are never recalculated."],
    v: "Load a file with ten million rows and confirm peak memory stays bounded, the first screen appears quickly, and final values match a full load. Kill the process mid-save and confirm the previous file is intact.",
    dep: ["part-readers-and-writers", "cell-blocks", "package-container"]
  },
  "delimited-text-import-export": {
    s: "Reads and writes comma, tab and other delimited text, with detection of encoding and separators, quoting rules, type inference and column-by-column control.",
    d: "Import guesses the encoding from a byte-order mark or content, the delimiter from sample lines, and the header row. It parses quoted fields that contain separators, line breaks and doubled quotes, then runs each field through the number parsing rules, with per-column overrides forcing text, dates in a given order or skipped columns. Export writes values as displayed or as stored, quotes fields where needed, and picks the line ending and encoding. A preview shows the guess before committing. Messy input is the norm: ragged rows, stray quotes and mixed encodings.",
    k: ["Type inference on import versus all text by default: inference is convenient, but corrupts identifiers and dates, while text-only requires manual conversion.", "Exporting displayed text versus raw values: displayed matches the screen, but loses precision and depends on locale."],
    p: ["A column of identifiers with leading zeros is converted to numbers on open, and the zeros are lost when saved again.", "Exported decimal commas collide with the comma delimiter when the delimiter is not quoted."],
    v: "Import a file with quoted line breaks, mixed quotes, a byte-order mark and ragged rows, compare cell contents against a reference parse, then export and re-import and confirm identical values for text columns.",
    dep: ["number-parsing-on-entry", "locale-and-calendar", "streaming-load-and-save"]
  },
  "round-trip-preservation": {
    s: "Keeps features, parts and attributes the application does not understand, so saving a file written by another program does not silently delete them.",
    d: "Real files contain extension elements, features from newer versions, and properties from other producers. A faithful editor either models them or stores them opaquely, such as retaining unknown elements with their position and re-emitting them. Opaque data must be kept consistent with edits: an unknown attribute on a row that is deleted is dropped with it, and a relationship to a part that is kept must remain valid. Where a feature cannot be supported, the file records a compatibility notice and the user is warned on save. The hard part is knowing which unknown content is safe to keep when its meaning depends on what changed around it.",
    k: ["Preserving unknown parts opaquely versus dropping them: preserving avoids data loss, but retained pieces may become inconsistent with edits.", "Warning on every lossy save versus silent best effort: warnings are honest, but are noisy for files that always have unsupported features."],
    p: ["Dropped extension elements cause another program to lose its custom data when the file is saved by this one.", "A preserved element that refers to a deleted row corrupts the file for the program that wrote it."],
    v: "Open files carrying features the application does not support, edit unrelated cells, save, and compare the package parts and attributes before and after: unrelated content must be unchanged and the unsupported feature must still work in the originating program.",
    dep: ["part-readers-and-writers", "package-container"]
  },
  "cached-values-on-load": {
    s: "Uses the computed results stored beside each formula in the file to show values at once, and decides when to trust them and when to recalculate.",
    d: "Files store each formula's last result alongside it, so a viewer or a loader that does not calculate can still show numbers. On load, the engine seeds cells with cached values, and either trusts them, recalculates everything, or recalculates only cells flagged as stale. A flag in the file records the producing application and whether a full recalculation was requested. Trusting the cache makes opening a huge workbook fast, but cached values from a different engine may differ in the last digits or for functions implemented differently. Volatile cells always recalculate. A file produced by a library that wrote no cached values has none and shows blanks until calculation.",
    k: ["Trusting cached values versus recalculating on open: trusting opens huge files quickly, but shows another engine's numbers until a recalculation.", "Writing cached values on save versus formulas only: writing makes files readable by non-calculating tools, but adds size and a chance of stale numbers."],
    p: ["A file written by a tool that does not calculate has empty cached values, and viewers show zeros or blanks.", "Trusted caches disagree with a changed function implementation, and numbers differ between users until someone recalculates."],
    v: "Open a large workbook with trusted caches and measure time to first values, then force a full recalculation and compare every cell. Open a file with no cached values and confirm results appear after calculation.",
    dep: ["part-readers-and-writers", "incremental-recalc", "volatile-functions"]
  },
  "autosave-and-recovery": {
    s: "Periodically saves the working state to a separate recovery store, and restores unsaved work after a crash or lost connection.",
    d: "In a desktop application, a timer writes a recovery copy, or a journal of changes since the last save, to a separate location, leaving the user's file untouched until an explicit save. After an abnormal exit, startup finds recovery files and offers them. In a collaborative service, every operation is persisted to the log immediately, and the client keeps pending operations locally to resend after reconnecting. Writing must not freeze the interface, so it runs on a background thread over a snapshot. A recovery copy should reproduce the exact state, including selection and undo history where practical. Stale recovery files need cleanup.",
    k: ["Full recovery snapshots versus an operation journal: snapshots are simple and robust, but costly for huge workbooks, while journals are cheap and need replay logic.", "Autosaving into the user's file versus a separate copy: saving in place is simple for the user, but overwrites the last good version with an unwanted change."],
    p: ["An autosave that blocks while serializing a large workbook freezes typing every few minutes.", "Recovery files for a crash loop are offered forever because they are never cleaned up."],
    v: "Edit, kill the process, restart, and confirm the recovery offer restores all edits made before the last autosave. Disconnect a collaborative client, edit offline, reconnect, and confirm every edit reaches the server once.",
    dep: ["streaming-load-and-save", "op-log-and-snapshots"]
  },
  "connection-manager": {
    s: "Holds the definitions of connections to databases, web services and files, with their queries and options, and mediates every access to them.",
    d: "A connection definition records a source type, location, query or table, parameters, refresh options and the destination of results, which may be a sheet table, a pivot cache or a data model. The manager opens connections through pluggable drivers, applies timeouts and size limits, and reports status. Parameters can come from cells, so a query changes with the sheet. Definitions are saved in the workbook, which means opening a file can trigger connection to arbitrary hosts, so permission prompts and allow lists matter. Query folding pushes filters to the source where possible so less data is transferred.",
    k: ["Pluggable drivers versus built-in connectors: plug-ins cover any source, but each is a code dependency with its own failures and security surface.", "Storing connection definitions in the workbook versus in user settings: in the workbook they travel with the file, but expose hosts, queries and parameters to every recipient."],
    p: ["Opening a workbook from an unknown sender triggers connections to hosts chosen by its author.", "A query parameter taken from a cell is concatenated into text and becomes an injection path."],
    v: "Define a connection with a parameter taken from a cell, refresh it, change the cell and confirm the data follows. Open the file in a restricted mode and confirm no connection is made without a prompt.",
    dep: ["connection-credential-handling", "query-refresh-scheduler"]
  },
  "query-refresh-scheduler": {
    s: "Runs connection refreshes on open, on a timer, on demand or on dependency changes, with concurrency limits, cancellation and retry.",
    d: "Refreshes are asynchronous jobs. A scheduler queues them with limits on concurrent connections, orders them by dependency when one query reads another's results, and runs them in the background so the workbook stays usable. Each job reports progress, supports cancellation, and on failure retries with backoff or leaves the previous data in place with an error status. Timer refresh in a closed workbook needs a server-side runner. Refresh completion marks the destination cells changed and triggers incremental recalculation once for the batch. Refresh storms from many open copies can overload a source, so server-side coalescing helps.",
    k: ["Background refresh versus blocking the workbook: background keeps the interface usable, but exposes partially updated data and complicates consistency.", "Refreshing per client versus once on the server: per client is simple, but multiplies the load on sources and gives users different data."],
    p: ["A hundred open copies each refresh every minute, and the source rate-limits or fails for everyone.", "A failed refresh clears the destination first and leaves an empty table that dependents then report on."],
    v: "Schedule a refresh with a failing source and confirm the previous data stays with an error status and a retry. Chain two dependent queries and confirm order. Open many copies and confirm the source receives one request per interval.",
    dep: ["connection-manager", "refresh-merge-and-cache", "incremental-recalc"]
  },
  "refresh-merge-and-cache": {
    s: "Writes fetched rows into the destination, preserving user columns, formats and formulas beside the data, and keeps a cached copy for offline use.",
    d: "A refresh produces a table of rows. The merge step replaces the destination's data region, keeping formulas typed beside it and formatting applied by column, and adjusts the table's extent when the row count changes. Options decide whether existing rows are replaced, appended or matched by key. Columns whose set or order changed at the source need mapping, or formulas that depend on them break. The result is cached in the file so the workbook opens with data and works offline. Large results stream into block storage, and refresh must be atomic from the viewpoint of recalculation, so dependents see a consistent table.",
    k: ["Replacing the whole data region versus matching rows by key: replacement is simple and always consistent, while key matching preserves per-row user edits but needs stable keys.", "Caching results in the file versus fetching on open: caching works offline and opens fast, but enlarges the file and can leak data to recipients."],
    p: ["The source renames a column, and formulas relying on the old column name quietly return errors or wrong values.", "Cached results carry confidential data to every person the workbook is sent to, even if they cannot access the source."],
    v: "Refresh from a source whose row count shrinks, grows and reorders columns, and confirm formulas beside the data, formats and table extent remain correct, with a clear mapping error for a vanished column.",
    dep: ["table-objects", "streaming-load-and-save", "connection-manager"]
  },
  "connection-credential-handling": {
    s: "Stores and supplies the identities used to reach data sources without writing secrets into the workbook, and decides whose identity each refresh uses.",
    d: "Credentials are held in a separate secure store tied to the user or the service, never inside the file; the workbook records only which kind of credential a connection needs. A refresh by a user runs as that user, and a scheduled server-side refresh runs as a stored service identity, with token refresh when the source uses expiring tokens. Sharing a workbook should not share access: recipients provide their own credentials or are denied. Prompts for credentials must come from the application and not from content in the file. Rotation and revocation must take effect on the next refresh.",
    k: ["Per-user credentials versus a shared service identity: per-user respects source permissions, but scheduled refresh then needs a designated user, while a service identity is simple and broadens access.", "Prompting at refresh versus stored tokens: prompts avoid storing secrets, but block unattended refresh."],
    p: ["A connection string with an embedded password is saved into the file and passed around by email.", "A shared scheduled refresh runs with the owner's identity and shows data to viewers who are not allowed to see it at the source."],
    v: "Save a workbook with a credentialed connection and inspect the package: no secrets must appear. Open it as another user and confirm they are asked for or denied credentials, and revoke a token and confirm the next refresh fails cleanly.",
    dep: ["sharing-roles", "file-encryption"]
  },
  "external-link-updating": {
    s: "Reads other workbooks referenced by formulas, refreshes the cached values of those links, and handles missing, moved or changed sources.",
    d: "Each external source has a link entry with a path or locator and a value cache per referenced range. Update reads the source through a lightweight reader without calculating or opening the interface, collects the referenced ranges, replaces the cache and marks dependents dirty. Modes include automatic with a prompt, manual and never. Sources on remote storage may need credentials. When the source cannot be found, the cached values stay and a status shows. Changing the source of a link rewrites the paths in all references to it. Security requires explicit consent before reading files named in a received workbook.",
    k: ["Automatic update on open with a prompt versus manual only: automatic keeps numbers current, but acts on paths supplied by the file's author.", "A lightweight reader versus a full engine for the source: lightweight is fast and safe, but cannot evaluate formulas that the source has no cached values for."],
    p: ["The source calculation mode is manual, so its cached values are stale and the link imports old numbers.", "Moving the folder breaks absolute paths, and values stay stale for months because the warning was dismissed."],
    v: "Link two workbooks, change a source cell and update, and confirm dependents change once. Move the source and confirm cached values stay with a visible broken status, then repoint the link and confirm recovery.",
    dep: ["external-workbook-refs", "part-readers-and-writers", "dirty-marking"]
  },
  "large-sheet-performance": {
    s: "Keeping memory, load time, recalculation and scrolling acceptable as sheets reach millions of rows and formulas, which shapes storage, rendering and calculation choices at once.",
    d: "Every layer has a scaling story. Storage must be sparse and block based, rendering must virtualize, formulas should be shared and compiled, and the dependency graph must use range nodes rather than per-cell edges. Whole-column references, volatile functions and per-cell lookups turn linear work into quadratic work without anyone noticing. Operations on the full sheet, such as sort, filter, structural edits and pivot refresh, need algorithmic care and cancellable background execution. Budgets such as first paint under a second, a keystroke response under a frame, and bounded memory per million cells need measurement on realistic worst-case files kept as regression tests.",
    k: ["Optimizing for typical small sheets versus the worst case: tuning for small sheets keeps code simple, but one huge file then makes the product unusable.", "Cancellable background operations versus blocking ones: background work keeps the interface alive, but forces every operation to cope with changes made while it runs."],
    p: ["A formula pattern that is linear on a thousand rows is quadratic on a million, and nobody notices until a customer file arrives.", "Performance fixes tuned on one benchmark shift the cost elsewhere, such as load time, without being measured."],
    v: "Keep a set of generated worst-case workbooks (dense, sparse, formula-heavy, wide) in a regression suite and record load time, peak memory, edit latency and scroll frame time for each build, failing the build on a budget breach.",
    dep: ["sparse-cell-storage", "grid-viewport-virtualization", "incremental-recalc", "range-dependency-index", "streaming-load-and-save"]
  },
  "calculation-determinism": {
    s: "The requirement that the same workbook and inputs produce the same results on every machine, thread count and run, which collaboration and caching both rely on.",
    d: "Sources of variation include thread scheduling in parallel sums, different evaluation order in cycles with iteration, floating-point library differences across platforms, locale-dependent text and date behavior, volatile functions and the time zone. In a collaborative workbook, clients and server each evaluate formulas, and if they disagree the visible numbers differ between people. Strategies include evaluating in one place and distributing results, fixing the order of reductions, using portable implementations of math functions, and treating time and random inputs as shared values supplied by the server. Determinism must be tested across configurations, not assumed.",
    k: ["Computing on the server and distributing values versus every client computing: one computer gives a single truth, but adds latency and server load, while client computation is responsive and can diverge.", "Fixed-order reductions versus fastest parallel order: fixed order gives identical bits, but gives up some speed."],
    p: ["Two collaborators see different totals because their platforms round a transcendental function differently in the last digit.", "A parallel sum accumulates in a different order each run and a cached comparison reports a change that never happened."],
    v: "Recalculate a function-heavy workbook on different platforms with one and many threads, repeat runs, and compare every cell bit for bit. Differences must be zero, or confined to an explicitly documented list of functions.",
    dep: ["multithreaded-recalc", "volatile-functions", "floating-point-and-display-rules", "collab-session-sync"]
  },
  "precision-and-compatibility": {
    s: "The decision to match the numeric behavior, edge-case results and quirks of established spreadsheet tools, rather than to be mathematically cleaner.",
    d: "Users move workbooks between tools and expect identical numbers, so many behaviors are inherited and not designed: the fifteen-digit display limit, the nonexistent leap day in the early date system, operator precedence for unary minus, text-to-number coercions, and function behavior on blanks. Each such rule is encoded in one place, with a test corpus of workbooks and expected outputs, rather than scattered. Deviations are made deliberately and documented, since a fix to an old bug is a change in someone's report. Imports and exports record which behavior set the file expects, so that new engines do not alter old results.",
    k: ["Bug-for-bug compatibility versus correct behavior: compatibility keeps existing workbooks and reports stable, but spreads known oddities into new code.", "A versioned behavior set per file versus one behavior for everything: versioning lets fixes ship without changing old files, but multiplies the code paths to test."],
    p: ["A cleanup of a numeric quirk changes totals in years-old financial models, and the product is blamed.", "Compatibility rules are implemented in several functions slightly differently, so the same edge case yields different results in each."],
    v: "Run a corpus of real workbooks with cached results from other tools through full recalculation, and compare every number. Every difference must be explained by a listed deliberate deviation.",
    dep: ["floating-point-and-display-rules", "argument-coercion-rules", "operator-precedence-and-intersection", "date-time-functions"]
  },
  "formulas-as-untrusted-code": {
    s: "The stance that anything inside a workbook from someone else, formulas, links, connections and scripts, may be hostile, and must run with limited authority.",
    d: "A formula can request external data, read other files through links, call scripts or run functions with side effects. Opened files can carry auto-run macros, connection definitions and links to attacker-chosen hosts. A safe design runs formulas as pure computation by default, asks before network, file and script access, runs scripts in a sandbox, and marks files from the network as restricted until the user opts in. Resource limits matter too: a formula can allocate a billion-cell array or recurse deeply, and a file can hold thousands of cells that each take seconds. Data injected into text functions can also be treated as formulas on export, called formula injection.",
    k: ["Restricted mode until the user opts in versus open by default: restricted mode blocks the common attacks, but trains users to click through the prompt.", "Resource limits on evaluation versus unlimited power: limits stop denial of service from crafted files, but reject some legitimate large models."],
    p: ["A delimited file exported from user input starts a cell with an equals sign and runs as a formula in the recipient's tool.", "A crafted array formula requests a trillion elements and exhausts memory before any limit check."],
    v: "Open a crafted workbook with an auto-run script, a link to an external host and a huge-array formula and confirm none acts without consent, the memory limit stops the array, and exported text that begins with an equals sign is neutralized.",
    dep: ["script-sandbox", "connection-manager", "external-workbook-refs", "formula-interpreter"]
  },
  "consistency-and-concurrency": {
    s: "The rules that decide how edits, recalculation, background tasks and remote operations interleave, so that every observer sees a state that was valid at some moment.",
    d: "Many actors touch the model: the user, a recalculation pass, background refreshes, scripts, sorts and remote collaborators. A single-writer model, where all changes are serialized through one command queue, makes reasoning simple, and the interface reads immutable snapshots or sees a consistent state between commands. Recalculation runs as part of a command or as a background step that publishes results atomically. Remote operations are transformed and applied as commands. Long operations are chunked or run against snapshots, and merged by transform. The danger is a half-applied command visible to a reader, such as a paste with values but not yet formats.",
    k: ["A single serialized command queue versus fine-grained locks: serialization is easy to reason about, but long commands block everything unless chunked.", "Readers on immutable snapshots versus reading live state: snapshots give consistent views for rendering and export, but cost memory for copy-on-write pieces."],
    p: ["A render reads cells while a paste is half applied and shows a mix of old and new values for one frame.", "A background refresh publishes rows while recalculation is mid-pass, and dependents compute from half the table."],
    v: "Run a stress test with concurrent edits, scripts, refreshes and remote operations while a checker repeatedly reads the model, and confirm every read equals the state after some prefix of the committed commands.",
    dep: ["undo-redo", "range-operational-transform", "incremental-recalc", "cell-blocks"]
  },
  "locale-independence": {
    s: "The rule that stored data and formulas use one neutral form while parsing, display and function names follow the user's locale, so a file means the same thing everywhere.",
    d: "Stored files keep numbers in a canonical decimal form, formulas in a canonical syntax with one argument separator and neutral function names, dates as serial numbers and formats in a canonical code. The locale applies at the edges: typed input parsing, number format rendering, formula display and entry, sorting and text comparison, and calendar names. Operations that depend on locale and silently differ between machines, such as text collation, case conversion and date text parsing, are explicit and documented. The test is a matrix: the same file opened under several locales must compute identical results and display locale-appropriate text.",
    k: ["Neutral storage with translation at the edges versus storing what the user typed: neutral storage is portable, but every edge needs reliable translation both ways.", "Locale-aware text functions versus ordinal behavior: locale-aware matches reader expectations, but makes results differ between machines unless the locale is part of the data."],
    p: ["A sort or comparison depends on the machine locale, so two collaborators see different orders for the same data.", "A function name translated in the display layer is saved in translated form and fails elsewhere."],
    v: "Open one workbook under at least four locales with different decimal marks, argument separators and date orders, and confirm identical computed values and locale-correct displays; save from each and confirm byte-identical stored formulas.",
    dep: ["locale-and-calendar", "formula-printer", "number-parsing-on-entry", "reference-notation"]
  },
});
